1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,540
Okay, moving on to trauma, epidural hematoma.

2
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:05,100
Epi means on top, dura refers to the dura,

3
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:06,800
and hematoma is a collection of blood.

4
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:09,000
So you get a collection of blood on top of the dura.

5
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,540
Now just remember, going back to normal,

6
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:12,680
here is the skull,

7
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,360
and remember that tightly attached to the skull is the dura.

8
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:18,760
Now this is important that it's tightly attached.

9
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:21,780
So in order to get a bleed that's going to be above the dura,

10
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,380
you're going to need to rupture a high-pressure circuit

11
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:27,000
so that it can separate the dura from the skull.

12
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:28,600
And so therefore, what's going to happen

13
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:29,740
is you're going to get a fracture.

14
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:33,200
This is going to rupture the middle meningeal artery,

15
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,260
artery because you need a high-pressure circuit,

16
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,100
and middle meningeal because that's the artery that runs here.

17
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:40,720
And that then is going to result in a collection of blood

18
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:42,420
between the skull and the dura.

19
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,360
So when you get this collection of blood,

20
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:46,460
it's going to look like this.

21
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:47,300
Here's the blood,

22
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:50,420
and of course the dura has been separated from the skull.

23
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:53,400
Now because the dura is tightly attached to the skull,

24
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:56,260
you're going to get these points at the edge of the bleed,

25
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,920
which are going to remain tightly attached,

26
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,300
and then you'll have the separation of the dura here from the skull.

27
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:06,360
And that's going to create the classic lens-shaped lesion on CT,

28
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:09,140
and you get a lens shape because it's attached here still

29
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,240
and still attached here.

30
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,900
And then what will happen over time is that blood will build up,

31
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:14,020
and as blood builds up,

32
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,120
the patient could be completely asymptomatic,

33
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,980
but all of a sudden the blood will sort of keep putting pressure on here

34
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:21,020
and eventually rupture this point or this point.

35
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:23,380
That will then further expand the hematoma,

36
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,420
and when you get an expansion of the hematoma,

37
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:27,320
that could then result in herniation,

38
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:28,700
which is a lethal complication.

39
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:31,340
And so this is also called the talk-and-die syndrome

40
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,500
because the patients can be asymptomatic for a few hours, for example,

41
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:37,500
and then all of a sudden this sort of ruptures or expands further,

42
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,100
and then that creates a lethal herniation.

43
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,420
So let's go back and read the text with that in mind.

44
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:43,760
Epidural hematoma,

45
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:46,400
you get a collection of blood between the dura and the skull,

46
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:50,940
fracture of the temporal bone with rupture of the middle meningeal artery,

47
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:52,160
particularly high yield.

48
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:55,480
You get a lens-shaped lesion on CT, particularly high yield.

49
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:58,840
You get a lucid interval that may precede the neurologic signs.

50
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:02,420
Again, you can have a few hours or even a day or so when you're totally fine,

51
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:03,660
and then all of a sudden, boom,

52
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:07,420
the hematoma expands as you sort of pull away at the dura one step further,

53
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:09,780
and then that can result in herniation,

54
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:11,180
which is a lethal complication.

55
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:14,320
Again, herniation refers to the pushing of one part of the brain

56
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:17,880
into another part of the brain, which we're going to talk about in a moment.

57
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:21,360
Subdural hematoma is when you have a collection of blood beneath the dura.

58
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,200
Now go back and remember that if this is the skull

59
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,260
and this is the dura tightly attached,

60
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:29,020
then underneath that is going to be the arachnoid,

61
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,300
and of course the arachnoid would then attach to the pia,

62
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:35,240
and the pia is directly connected to the brain.

63
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:38,980
And so what's going to happen here is you're going to get a bleed in this space,

64
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:42,680
and that's called a subdural hematoma underneath the dura.

65
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:45,680
Now this is usually due to rupture of a bridging vein.

66
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:48,160
The way I remember this is that you don't need a lot of pressure

67
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:51,300
to be able to rip through these little arachnoid-like spider webs,

68
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:53,540
and again arachnoid kind of also means spider,

69
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:55,300
and it usually arises with trauma.

70
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:58,280
Now remember here are these bridging veins like this,

71
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,940
and so these can easily rupture, especially with trauma,

72
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:02,900
resulting in a subdural hematoma.

73
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,200
At the same time, when there's atrophy of the brain,

74
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,940
these veins are going to be stretched as the brain is pulled away, for example,

75
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:12,820
and then there's increased risk of rupture with atrophy.

76
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:17,380
And so your text here says that a subdural hematoma is a collection of blood beneath the dura,

77
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:22,320
tearing of bridging veins, high yield, and it usually arises with trauma.

78
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:24,540
Here's a picture of a subdural hematoma.

79
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:26,720
This thick layer here represents the dura,

80
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:29,140
and then you can see that there's blood underneath the dura,

81
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:31,280
and it's sitting on top of the brain here.

82
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:34,040
So again, classic subdural hematoma.

83
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,380
This will present with progressive neurologic signs.

84
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,200
As the blood slowly builds up and spreads across the surface of the brain,

85
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:42,040
you'll get progressive neurologic signs,

86
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:44,480
and again herniation would be a lethal complication.

87
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:47,600
There's no space in that box for anything but the brain,

88
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:50,460
and so when you add blood, you can eventually cause a herniation.

89
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:53,380
Now again, herniation is displacement of brain tissue.

90
00:03:53,860 --> 00:03:56,580
It's due to mass effect or increased intracranial pressure.

91
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,700
I'm going to use this simple picture to illustrate the three most common types of herniation.

92
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,900
The first is a tonsillar herniation.

93
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:04,240
This is relatively straightforward.

94
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:07,120
You get herniation of the cerebellar tonsils,

95
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:10,440
which is right here through the foramen magnum,

96
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:12,540
which then compresses the brainstem,

97
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:14,320
and when this compresses the brainstem,

98
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:16,160
you can get cardiopulmonary arrest.

99
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:20,000
So this first herniation is a tonsillar herniation.

100
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:23,060
The next herniation is called a subfalcine herniation.

101
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,660
This is when you get the cingulate gyrus, which is present here,

102
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:30,180
to herniate underneath the phalx cerebri, which is present here.

103
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:33,520
Now the problem here is that you'll compress the anterior cerebral artery,

104
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:35,180
which will then lead to infarction.

105
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,120
The third type of herniation is an uncoherniation.

106
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:41,980
This is when you get herniation of the uncus of the temporal lobe,

107
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:42,920
which is right here,

108
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:46,940
into the area between the cerebellum and the brainstem here,

109
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,520
which is also called the tentorum cerebelli.

110
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:52,400
Now the problem is that you're going to then compress three structures.

111
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,520
Number one, you're going to compress cranial nerve three,

112
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:58,640
and that's going to result in the eyes moving down and out with a dilated pupil.

113
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,220
You'll also compress the posterior cerebral artery,

114
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:03,560
which will give you infarction of the occipital lobe,

115
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:08,640
and then you can also pull on the paramedian artery that's running along the brainstem,

116
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:11,660
and that'll give you direct hemorrhages on the brainstem.

117
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:14,800
And so these are the three complications of an uncoherniation.

118
00:05:15,340 --> 00:05:16,820
Moving on to demyelinating disorders,

119
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:21,280
recall that the axons of both the central and the peripheral nervous system are myelinated.

120
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:25,060
The purpose of the myelin is to improve conduction across the axons.

121
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:26,600
In the central nervous system,

122
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:30,040
the key cell that myelinates the axons is the oligodendrocyte,

123
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:31,720
and in the peripheral nervous system,

124
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:34,540
the key cell that myelinates the axons is the Schwann cell.

125
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:36,260
In a demyelinating disorder,

126
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:39,680
you get destruction of the myelin or the oligodendrocytes.

127
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:41,980
And again, here we're going to focus on the central nervous system,

128
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:43,540
so the oligodendrocytes.

129
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,300
In this particular case, the axons are preserved,

130
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:47,900
so you don't damage the axons,

131
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:52,680
but the conduction of the impulse across those axons is going to be impaired.

132
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,520
The first of these disorders is called leukodystrophies.

133
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:56,460
Leuco means white,

134
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:59,560
and dystrophy means that there's a problem within the white matter.

135
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:02,040
Of course, the white matter is white within the brain

136
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:03,560
because it's loaded with myelin,

137
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:05,420
and that's what gives it the white color.

138
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:08,240
And so leukodystrophies are inherited mutations

139
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:12,520
in enzymes necessary for the production or for the maintenance of myelin.

140
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,300
The first of these is metachromatic leukodystrophy.

141
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:18,360
This is when you have a deficiency of aural sulfatase.

142
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:20,380
It's the most common leukodystrophy,

143
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:23,160
and the problem here is that you can't degrade myelin.

144
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:26,420
Now recall that you produce and turn over myelin on a regular basis,

145
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:28,380
and so if you can't destroy the myelin,

146
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:30,000
that will result in a leukodystrophy.

147
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:32,960
And in this particular case, the myelin cannot be degraded,

148
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:35,500
and so it accumulates within the lysosomes.

149
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:40,720
Krabbe's disease is when you have deficiency of galactocerebroside beta-galactosidase,

150
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:46,420
and this then results in the accumulation of galactocerebroside within the macrophages.

151
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:51,040
In adrenal leukodystrophy, you have a problem with both the adrenal gland and the white matter.

152
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:55,020
This is due to impaired addition of coenzyme A to long-chain fatty acids.

153
00:06:55,020 --> 00:07:00,220
The fatty acids then accumulate, damaging both the adrenal gland and the white matter.

154
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:05,020
Multiple sclerosis is autoimmune destruction of the CNS myelin and the oligodendrocytes.

155
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:10,440
Very high yield to know that it's the oligodendrocytes that are getting destroyed in multiple sclerosis.

156
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,320
This happens to be the most common chronic CNS disease of young adults.

157
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,820
Women are more commonly affected than men.

158
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:20,840
Now again, it's an autoimmune disease, and remember the general principle with autoimmune disease

159
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:25,660
is that you have a genetically susceptible individual that sees an environmental trigger.

160
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:29,820
And so the genetic susceptibility here is the association with HLA-DR2,

161
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:34,680
and the environmental trigger is that it's more commonly seen in regions away from the equator,

162
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:37,160
and that's also particularly high yield to know.

163
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:41,520
The clinical features of multiple sclerosis are dependent on the region that's involved.

164
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,820
So if the optic nerve is involved, you can have blurry vision in one eye.

165
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:48,400
If the brainstem is involved, the patients can have vertigo and scanning speech.

166
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:51,220
This scanning speech is particularly important in high yield,

167
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:54,620
and that's because this speech will resemble the speech of someone who's drunk,

168
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:57,860
but of course they're not drunk, they simply have multiple sclerosis.

169
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:04,300
And then involvement of the medial longitudinal fasciculus gives you something called internuclear ophthalmoplegia.

170
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:07,700
Now this is relatively high yield because it goes back to neuroanatomy,

171
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:09,740
so let's take a moment to illustrate this.

172
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:11,820
Here's the left eye and the right eye,

173
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:16,280
and of course you've got muscles coming off these eyes here.

174
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:20,400
This is the lateral rectus muscle, medial rectus.

175
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:24,080
This is the medial rectus on this side and the lateral rectus.

176
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:30,240
And what you need to know is that cranial nerve 6 directly attaches to the lateral rectus,

177
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,640
and so here's the nucleus of cranial nerve 6 directly attaching to the lateral rectus.

178
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:40,580
Now the medial rectus, you've got here cranial nerve 3 that sort of feeds the medial rectus,

179
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:44,320
and so this will directly feed the medial rectus on either side.

180
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:49,640
And then there'll be a crossover to the cranial nerve 6 on each side,

181
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:55,040
and what is present between this crossover is something called the medial longitudinal fasciculus here

182
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:57,640
and another medial longitudinal fasciculus here.

183
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:00,640
Now let's say that the patient wants to look to the left,

184
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:03,620
so you want to move this eye this way and this eye this way.

185
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:06,640
Well, what's going to happen is this cranial nerve 6 is going to fire,

186
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,220
and when it fires, it's going to drag the lateral rectus down, pulling the eye to the left,

187
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:14,960
and at the same time it's going to send a message this way,

188
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:18,940
and it's going to fire cranial nerve 3, which will drag this medial rectus down,

189
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:21,360
which will then pull the right eye to the left.

190
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:24,700
Now imagine if the medial longitudinal fasciculus is damaged,

191
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:29,120
then when you try to look to the left, you'll be able to pull the left eye to the left.

192
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:34,000
However, when you send this message up this way, it's going to be blocked because of the damage here,

193
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:36,600
and you won't be able to pull the right eye to the left.

194
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:39,920
And so this is called intranuclear ophthalmoplegia.

195
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:44,200
Additional clinical features include involvement of the cerebral white matter,

196
00:09:44,340 --> 00:09:47,680
which can result in hemiparesis or unilateral loss of sensation.

197
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:52,560
The spinal cord can be involved, which would result in lower extremity loss of sensation or weakness.

198
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:55,500
And then, of course, the autonomic nervous system can be involved,

199
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:57,960
giving you bowel, bladder, and sexual dysfunction.

200
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:02,700
Now the diagnosis of multiple sclerosis is made by MRI, and it will show plaques.

201
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:06,700
You can also do a lumbar puncture, and it would show increased lymphocytes,

202
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:11,200
increased immunoglobulin with oligoclonal immunoglobulin bands.

203
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:15,960
That's high yield, oligoclonal immunoglobulin bands, and then myelin basic protein,

204
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:20,600
and that's because you've destroyed myelin, so myelin basic protein will be present in the CSF.

205
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:24,840
Here's a picture of a brain, and just remember that, of course, you've got gray matter and you've got white matter.

206
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:29,520
And the gray matter is gray because there is no myelin, and the white matter is white because there is myelin.

207
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:34,220
So when you get demyelination, what's going to happen is you're going to get gray areas within the white matter.

208
00:10:34,540 --> 00:10:38,680
So, for example, this is a region here where you've got graying of the white matter,

209
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,580
and this represents multiple sclerosis.

210
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:45,260
The treatment of multiple sclerosis will be high-dose steroids during an acute attack,

211
00:10:45,620 --> 00:10:50,460
and interferon beta can be given, which has been shown to slow the progression of the disease.

212
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:53,820
The next disorder is subacute sclerosing panencephalitis.

213
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:56,320
Encephalitis means that you've got inflammation of the brain,

214
00:10:56,420 --> 00:10:59,580
and the word pan tells you that it's involving both the gray and the white matter.

215
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:03,400
This is a progressive debilitating encephalitis that leads to death,

216
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:09,420
and very high yield to know that it's due to a slowly progressing, persistent infection of the brain by measles virus.

217
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:13,440
So this is a slow, persistent infection by measles virus.

218
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,140
Infection actually occurs in infancy, and then the damage takes time

219
00:11:17,140 --> 00:11:20,160
until eventually the neurologic signs arise years later.

220
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:22,500
It's going to be characterized by viral inclusions.

221
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:26,600
Again, this is due to measles virus within both the gray matter and the white matter.

222
00:11:26,720 --> 00:11:30,380
Again, you've got a panencephalitis, and so when it's in the white matter,

223
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:33,060
that's going to be damaging the oligodendrocytes.

224
00:11:33,700 --> 00:11:40,040
Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is an encephalopathy that involves the white matter at multiple foci,

225
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:43,620
and it's due to JC virus infection of oligodendrocytes.

226
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:46,040
It's very high yield to note that it's JC virus infection.

227
00:11:46,040 --> 00:11:49,160
Now, the patient actually has a latent JC virus infection,

228
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:52,780
but it's immunosuppression that leads to reactivation of the virus.

229
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:55,800
So, for example, AIDS can lead to reactivation of the virus.

230
00:11:56,200 --> 00:11:59,140
Patients with leukemia who may be immunosuppressed, for example,

231
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:01,400
can get reactivation of the virus as well.

232
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:06,060
This presents with rapidly progressive neurologic signs that eventually lead to death.

233
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:09,360
And the final disorder is central pontine myelinolysis.

234
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:13,740
This is when you get focal demyelination of the pons, hence the word pontine,

235
00:12:13,740 --> 00:12:19,560
and it occurs in the center of the pons, and it's due to rapid IV correction of hyponatremia,

236
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:24,860
particularly in an alcoholic or a patient with liver disease or someone who is severely malnourished.

237
00:12:25,340 --> 00:12:26,620
And that's what I've stated here.

238
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:31,040
You get myelinolysis, which means that you start destroying the white matter within the pons.

239
00:12:31,320 --> 00:12:34,440
Let's go back and remind ourselves that if this is sort of the cortex of the brain,

240
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:40,060
and then everything is coming down into a brainstem, remember that everybody is sort of feeding into this highway.

241
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:45,380
So all of the axons that are going to be running through this system are all going to come through the pons.

242
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:50,620
And so one of the consequences of central pontine myelinolysis, and particularly high yield for board exams,

